          "task1": "The line graph illustrates the proportion of households which had access to the internet in Canada, Brazil and India from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, internet access rose steadily in all three countries over the period. Canada had the highest figures throughout, while India had the lowest, although it showed the biggest growth at the end.\n\nIn 2000, 42% of Canadian homes were connected, which was more than double the figure for Brazil (18%) and much higher than India, where only 9% of households had access. Over the next ten years, Canada increased to 66% and Brazil rose quickly to 47%. India grew more slowly and reached 24% in 2010.\n\nFrom 2010 to 2020, the growth continued in every country. Canada reached 82% by the end of the period, and Brazil climbed to 72%, so the gap between them became smaller. The most dramatic change was in India, where the percentage jumped from 38% in 2015 to 55% in 2020, although it was still the lowest of the three countries.",
          "task2": "Nowadays more and more companies allow their staff to work from home, especially after the pandemic. Some people think this arrangement is good for both workers and employers. I mostly agree with this opinion, although I believe there are some disadvantages that should be considered.\n\nOn the one hand, working from home has clear benefits for employees. First of all, they do not need to commute every day, so they save a lot of time and money. For example, in big cities like Dhaka, people can spend two or three hours in traffic, and this time could be used for family or rest. In addition, a flexible schedule helps workers to balance their work and personal life, which can make them more satisfied with their job.\n\nOn the other hand, employers can also gain from remote work. Companies can reduce their overheads because they need smaller offices and pay less for electricity and rent. Moreover, they can recruit talented people from any location, not only from the city where the office is. This means the talent pool is much bigger than before.\n\nHowever, there are some drawbacks. Some employees feel isolated when they work alone for a long time, and junior staff may lose the chance to learn from experienced colleagues. Also, the boundary between work and home can become unclear, so people might work longer hours and suffer from burnout.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe working from home benefits both employees and employers in many ways, such as saving time and reducing costs. Nevertheless, a hybrid model, where staff come to the office some days, may be the best solution to avoid isolation."
