      "band": 6.0,
      "durationSec": 2280,
      "data": {
        "text": "Over the past decade, remote work has moved from a rare perk to a mainstream arrangement for many office staff. In my view, working from home offers very good things to both workers and companies, although it is not without drawbacks.\n\nFor employees, the most obvious advantage is the time saved on commuting. In a city such as Dhaka, where a single journey can take more than an hour, people who works from home can spend this time with their kids or get some rest before starting work. This tends to improve concentration during working hours. Also, workers are happier. Also they stay longer in the same company.\n\nIn the other hand, employers benefit from lower office costs, which mean they can invest more in staff training. A company that no longer rents a big office in the city centre can save a lot of money every year. Moreover, managers can hire talented staff from other cities, which widens the pool of applicants.\n\nAdmittedly, some workers feel lonely at home, and it is really difficult for new staff to learn from their colleagues. For instance, a junior accountant may not know who to ask when a question appears. However, regular video meetings and one office day per week can solve most of these difficulties.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that working from home benefits both employees and employers. If companies organise it carefully, the advantages are much greater than the disadvantages, and both sides will be more productive and more satisfied in the long term.",
